  "answer_to_user": "Using the ~4.3% 10-year Treasury as your hurdle, I screened for a MEANINGFUL spread above it (yield >= 6%, i.e. at least ~1.7pts more than bonds) AND layered on safety gates — payout ratio <= 80% and an 8-10 year no-cut streak — because extra equity yield is only worth it if it isn't a trap. Results that clear both bars: PennantPark Floating Rate (PFLT.US) ~15.9% at ~33% payout, 10-yr streak; Bain Capital Specialty Finance (BCSF.US) ~13.9% at ~80% payout, 8-yr streak; Reach PLC (RCH.LSE) ~13.7% at ~47% payout, 10-yr streak; Western Union (WU.US) ~12.9% at ~64% payout, 10-yr streak; Spok Holdings (SPOK.US) ~12.1% at a very low ~2% payout, 8-yr streak; GIIR Inc (035000.KO) ~11.2% at ~48% payout, 10-yr streak. The critical framing: unlike the Treasury, none of this yield is contractual — a stock can cut its dividend AND the price can fall, so the 'spread' compensates you for price risk and cut risk that a bond doesn't carry. That's exactly why I gated on payout and streak instead of just picking the highest yields; several of these (PFLT, WU, RCH) are BDCs/high-payout structures where you'd still want to confirm cash-flow coverage. So: yes, there are names yielding meaningfully above 4.3% with reasonable payout and clean multi-year records — but 'higher than bonds' only counts if the extra yield is safe, and even these carry risks a Treasury doesn't. Not a specific allocation recommendation."
